---
import LetsTalk from '@components/home/LetsTalk.astro'
import Insight from '@components/insights/Insight.astro'
import InsightSignoff from '@components/insights/InsightSignoff.astro'
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import { getInsightNav } from '@data/insights'
import InsightNav from '@components/insights/InsightNav.astro'

const { previous, current, next } = getInsightNav('how-to-choose-an-ai-consulting-service-for-small-businesses')
---

<Layout title={current?.title} description={current?.summary}>
  <Insight tags={current?.tags}>
    <span slot="insight-title">{current?.title}</span>
    <span slot="insight-subtitle">{current?.dateLabel}</span>
    <span slot="insight-text">
      <p>
        Most small businesses do not have an AI problem. They have a bookkeeping problem, a customer enquiry problem, or
        a quoting problem, and someone has suggested AI might fix it. A good consultant starts there. Here is what to look
        for when you are deciding who to bring in:
      </p><br />
      <ul class="lg:mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They start with your business, not with tools.</strong> The first conversation should be about what is
            slow, expensive or error-prone in how you operate. If it opens with a product demo, they have already decided
            what to sell you.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They can tell you when AI is the wrong answer.</strong> Ask them directly for an example where they
            advised a client not to use AI. Anyone who cannot answer is selling, not consulting.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They have built and run something, not just advised on it.</strong> Ask to see a working system they
            delivered and what it costs the client to run today. A strategy deck is easy to produce. A tool that still works
            six months later is not.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They explain where your data goes in plain language.</strong> Which vendor sees your customer records,
            what happens if that vendor changes its pricing or terms, and whether there are options that keep the data with
            you. If the answer is vague, assume the worst.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They quote running costs, not just build costs.</strong> API usage, subscriptions, and the maintenance
            needed when a model changes or gets retired. Most small business AI projects fail on the ongoing bill nobody
            mentioned.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They propose a small, fixed piece of work first.</strong> One clear outcome, a defined scope, and a
            price. An open retainer to "explore AI" is a good deal for the consultant and rarely for you.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They are clear about who owns what at the end.</strong> Prompts, workflows, integrations and the
            accounts they sit in. Too many small businesses find the automation lives in a consultant's personal login after
            the engagement ends.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-3 ml-2 lg:ml-4 lg:my-1">
          <span class="shrink-0 w-3 h-3 mt-[2.5px] lg:mt-1.25" style={`background-color: var(--accent-bgc);`}></span>
          <span>
            <strong>They know more than one platform.</strong> A consultant who only knows ChatGPT, or who resells one
            vendor's product, will recommend that regardless of fit. Ask what else they considered and why they ruled it
            out.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <br />
      <p>
        None of this means you need a large firm or a long engagement. Some of the best outcomes we have seen come from a
        few days of honest <a href="/services/consulting" class="underline underline-offset-2">consulting</a> that ends with "do this one thing, and do not bother with the rest". The point is to
        hire someone who will give you that answer even when it means less work for them.
      </p><br />
      <InsightSignoff />
    </span>
  </Insight>

  <InsightNav previousInsight={previous} nextInsight={next} />

  <LetsTalk config={{ buttons: 'both', primaryCta: 'message' }} />
</Layout>
